15 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt (Smilax glabra plant image showing red berries and green leaves next to images of its rhizome and rhizome extract. Graphs B and C display mass spectrometry results with peaks indicated along a time scale. Section D presents chemical structures of six compounds: Astilbin, Engelitin, Isoastilbin, Neoastilbin, Neoisostilbin, and Isoengelitin, with structural formulas detailed beneath each name.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt (Graphs A and B show a linear decrease of residual activity percentage with increasing SGR concentration, with IC50 values of 1.04 mg/mL and 1.01 mg/mL, respectively. Graphs C and D display a curved decrease in residual activity percentage with increasing AHA concentration, with IC50 values of 4.93 µg/mL and 1.56 µg/mL, respectively. Dashed lines indicate 50% residual activity.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Graphs A and B depict Lineweaver-Burk plots showing different concentrations of an inhibitor affecting enzyme activity, indicated by changes in slope and intercept. Graphs a, b, c, and d display linear relationships between systematic growth rate \(SGR\) and slope or intercept, illustrating the effect of inhibitor concentrations. Different symbols represent varying inhibitor levels: 2.0, 1.0, 0.5, and 0.0 mg/mL.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 52 0 R /Alt (Graphs labeled A to D show ammonia concentration \(mM\) over time \(minutes\) with varying SGR concentrations \(0.0 to 2.0 mg/mL\). Each graph displays increasing linear trends. Error bars indicate variability.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 53 0 R /Alt (Bar graphs labeled A to D display residual activity percentages for different combinations of HPU and JBU conditions with various additives. Error bars indicate variability, with significance marked by asterisks and hashtags. Each graph compares a baseline with multiple treatments, showing varying impacts on residual activity.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 41 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 54 0 R /Alt (Bar charts labeled A to D depict residual activity percentages for different conditions over time intervals of five, ten, twenty, and forty minutes. Each chart uses a color-coded legend. The x-axis lists various combinations such as HPU, JBU, SGR, and additives like DTT and GSH, while the y-axis measures residual activity in percentage. Significant changes are marked with asterisks.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 42 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 55 0 R /Alt (Line graphs labeled A and B display residual activity percentage over incubation time in minutes. Both graphs show that the residual activity slowly increased from 20% to approximately 40% over time after the introduction of GSH at 20 minutes of incubation. Error bars indicate variability.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 42 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 56 0 R /Alt <> /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 57 0 R /Alt (The image consists of four graphs. Graph A shows a Lineweaver-Burk plot for various concentrations of Astilbin with inverse urea concentration on the x-axis and inverse velocity on the y-axis. Graph B is similar, but with a larger scale on the y-axis. Graphs a and c depict linear relationships between Astilbin concentration and slope values, while graphs b and d show the relationship between Astilbin concentration and intercept values. Each graph is labeled respectively and includes data points with linear trend lines.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 58 0 R /Alt (Molecular graphics illustrate interactions between proteins and ligands, with eight panels labeled A to H. Each panel shows a protein surface model connected to a zoomed-in view of a ligand-binding site, highlighting the alignment and interaction details. Different colors and structures represent various protein and ligand components, emphasizing their spatial relationships.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
